# <PROJECT_NAME> — Copilot Instructions (Repository Router)

> **What this is.** This file is auto-loaded into EVERY GitHub Copilot interaction in this repo — inline completions, Chat, Code Review, Cloud Agent. Keep it thin (under 200 lines). It's a router, not a manual.

## Project identity

<PROJECT_NAME> is an iOS application (`<XCODE_SCHEME>` scheme in `<XCODE_WORKSPACE>`) targeting iOS <IOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET>+. <ONE_SENTENCE_PRODUCT_DESCRIPTION>

## Universal evidence rule — "no evidence, no claim"

If a claim cannot be tied to a `file:line`, `Info.plist:key`, schema, App Store guideline, or test output, mark it as `confidence: low` (outbound) or move to `unverified_claims` (inbound). Hallucinations don't pass the orchestrator's validation.

## Golden rules (always)

1. **Never push to `main` or production without project-owner approval.**
2. **Always start on `<DEFAULT_BASE_BRANCH>`.** Run `<BUILD_COMMAND>` before commit.
3. **Don't refactor outside the task.** Preserve existing behavior unless asked.
4. **Read the matching `.github/instructions/<NAME>.instructions.md` before editing.** Auto-loaded by `applyTo:` glob; if you see one in context, read its Hard rules first.
5. **Never commit signing material:** `.cer`, `.p12`, `.mobileprovision`, `*.keychain-db`, anything matching `secret`/`credentials`/`private-key`. Use the `/commit-push-pr` skill — it refuses to stage these.
6. **For privacy-touching changes** (Info.plist, entitlements, third-party SDK additions), `ios-privacy` REVIEW-ONLY agent must audit before merge.
7. **`@MainActor` discipline** — anything mutating `@Published` / `@State` / UIView state runs on main. Use `await MainActor.run { }` for async returns.
8. **Multi-agent handoffs are bidirectional and structured.** Orchestrator → specialist uses `handoff:` YAML. Specialist → orchestrator uses `return:` YAML. See `docs/ai-context/HANDOFF_SCHEMA.md`. **No evidence, no claim.**
9. **Corrections harden into instruction files.** If the owner corrected a *recurring* pattern this turn, end the turn by drafting a one-line patch to the right `.github/instructions/<domain>.instructions.md` and ask for approval. Never say "I'll remember" — any memory the IDE or CLI keeps is machine-local and invisible to every other agent and teammate.
10. **Deferred work must be written, not spoken.** Any "later" / follow-up / known gap named in a summary is appended to `docs/<AREA>_BACKLOG.md` (what / why / effort / revisit-when) before the turn ends.
11. **Every production push freshens the docs — same turn, no exceptions.** A push to `main`, an App Store submission or an external-TestFlight promotion updates `<CHANGELOG_PATH>` (the anchor), the affected orientation map(s), the affected instruction files, and `docs/ai-context/PROJECT.md` §3 if what is live on the App Store / TestFlight changed. (Enforced by the `agentStop` doc-freshness hook if `.github/hooks/` is installed — see `.github/hooks/*.json` and the framework's `docs/10-MECHANICAL-ENFORCEMENT.md`.)
12. **One name per concept.** `docs/ai-context/GLOSSARY.md` is a rule file; adding a new name for an existing concept is a defect.

## Mandatory workflow (every non-trivial task)

1. Restate the task.
2. Identify affected specialist(s) (UI / data / network / tests / release / etc.).
3. Read `docs/ai-context/PROJECT.md` (current truth — especially §3, what is live where: App Store / TestFlight / backend) and `docs/ai-context/INDEX.md`. Skim `docs/ai-context/LEARNINGS.md` §D (agent corrections) before the first edit.
4. Read the minimum orientation map(s) flagged for this task (each ~50-150 lines).
5. **For each file you plan to edit, look up matching `.github/instructions/*.instructions.md` and read it.**
6. Use a specialist agent (`@<SPECIALIST_NAME>`) only when useful. For medium/complex cross-domain tasks, use `@<PROJECT_SLUG>-orchestrator`.
7. Inspect code before proposing changes.
8. Smallest safe change.
9. Run relevant checks (`xcodebuild test`, snapshot tests, accessibility audit per task). Verification follows the proof ladder in `.github/skills/<PROJECT_SLUG>-engineering/SKILL.md` (a green build is evidence, not proof).
10. **Definition of done:** report changed files, checks run with results, risks, follow-ups (written to a backlog), AND the list of `.github/instructions/*.instructions.md` files read before editing.

